🧠 What is Abstraction in Java?
Abstraction is one of the four core OOP concepts (others: Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism).
It means hiding complex internal implementation details and showing only the essential features of an object.

🎯 Simple Definition:
Abstraction lets you focus on what an object does, not how it does it.

📦 Real-Life Example:
A car has methods like start(), drive(), stop().
You don't care how the engine starts — you just use start().

🧱 In Java, Abstraction is achieved using:
1. Abstract Classes
Can have abstract (unimplemented) and concrete (implemented) methods.

Cannot be instantiated.

java
Copy
Edit
abstract class Animal {
    abstract void makeSound(); // abstract method

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating"); // concrete method
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}
2. Interfaces
Fully abstract (Java 7)

Can have default, static, and private methods (Java 8+)

java
Copy
Edit
interface Vehicle {
    void drive(); // abstract by default
}

class Car implements Vehicle {
    public void drive() {
        System.out.println("Car is driving");
    }
}
✅ Benefits of Abstraction:
Hides implementation logic

Improves code readability and reusability

Helps in achieving loose coupling

Encourages design flexibility (plug-and-play implementation)